Abstract
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are good absorbents that provide high specific surface area, modified pore surface and controllable pore size. The aim of this work is to prepare a MOFs material with good toluene adsorption property in the presence of water. In this paper, modified UiO-66 (University of Oslo 66) was successfully synthesized with polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) as structure-directing agent by a simple solvothermal method. The physical and chemical properties were obtained by a series of characterization instruments. Some missing-linker defect sites were observed on modified materials (defective UiO-66) and were known as the main active sites for toluene adsorption. The defective UiO-66 (PVP-U-0.5, $259\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}\cdot$ ) demonstrated 1.7 times toluene adsorption capacity of the pristine UiO-66 $(151~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1},$ ) when the $\mathrm{PVP/Zr^{4+}}$ ratio was 0.5. The interactions between toluene and UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 were assessed through the Henry’s law constant $\mathrm{(K_{H})}$ and the isosteric adsorption heat $\mathrm{(\DeltaAH_{ads})}$ , which indicated that stronger interaction between PVP-U-0.5 and toluene molecules. Moreover, PVP-U-0.5 displayed good adsorption capacity $(84~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}})$ at high relative humidity ( $70\%$ RH). Water temperature programmed desorption experiments revealed that PVP-U-0.5 had more hydrophobic property, which provided a further possibility for practical application for the removal of toluene.
Keywords: Toluene; adsorption; PVP; UiO-66; humid air.
## 1. Introduction
Recently, more and more environmental contaminants have attracted the attention [1-10]. Among them, volatile organic compounds (VOCs) are a major class of gaseous pollutants and widely present in industrial emission streams [11, 12]. Especially toluene and benzene are the typical example of such pollutants. The effective VOCs removal has attracted the interest of many researchers all over the world, because of the urgency of protecting the environment and human health. Over the past few decades, quantities of researchers have developed some advanced technologies for the treatment of VOCs, mainly including catalytic combustion[13-16], photocatalytic processes[12, 17, 18], and absorption[19-23]. Catalytic combustion usually requires high temperature and additional reactants; thus, it is suitable for emission control of power plants, steel refineries, and petrochemical factories. There are some challenges in photocatalytic processes, since the utilization rate of light is still low and the development of processing equipment is not mature enough. On the other hand, absorption method toward VOC removal by some regular materials (zeolites, activated carbon et al.) is simpler and more effective when the VOCs concentration of below 1000 ppm [21]. Low equipment investment and energy consumption are required for adsorption method, which can be easily applied in industrial fields. Therefore, the research on the development of adsorbents for VOC removal has increased with increasingly stringent regulations on VOCs emission limits.
Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), which composed of organic ligands and metal ions, have received much attention due to highly ordered and tunable structures, and dense aromatic linkers [24-27]. MOFs can be applied in many areas, such as photochemical catalysis [28-33], adsorption [34-40], and catalysis [41-46]. Many MOFs, such as MIL-101 (Cr), Cu-BTC, ZIF-67, UiO-66, and MIL-101 (Fe), are used as adsorbents to remove various VOCs [22, 35, 37, 39, 40]. However, most studies have focused on the VOCs adsorption properties of MOFs and ignored the water resistance. Based on the target, this work has been performed aiming at developing more effective and selective adsorbent materials for toluene removal in the presence of water.
At present, owing to remarkable mechanical, chemical, water and thermal stability, zirconium-based MOFs UiO-66 becomes potential adsorbent [47, 48]. Previous studies indicated that modification of UiO-66 material could improve toluene adsorption performance [49]. During this process, structure-directing agent was very important. Recently, polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) is widely used as a surfactant to control the morphology, shape, size and as a stabilizer due to its steric effect [50, 51]. Moreover, PVP is a common polymer with non-toxicity, excellent bio-compatibility, excellent wetting properties, and good complexation ability [52]. It can be easily dissolved in water and other polar solvents. Therefore, in this paper, modified UiO-66 was successfully synthesized with PVP as structure-directing agent by a simple solvothermal method. The single-component and competitive adsorption breakthrough curves (The change curve of adsorption column outlet concentration in the adsorption process) were measured by GC gas chromatograph. The schematic diagram of adsorption breakthrough curve was shown in Fig. S1. The adsorption capacity was determined by calculating breakthrough curves areas according to the following formula. Different analytical techniques were employed to characterize the samples. The paper also reported the adsorption behaviors when adsorption was affected by environmental factors such as high temperature and humidity.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Synthesis
Hydrothermal synthesis of UiO-66[48]: A total of $0.053\ \mathrm{g}$ of zirconium chloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4}}$ , $0.227\mathrm{mmol},$ ) and $0.034\ \mathrm{g}$ of terephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},0.227\mathrm{mmol})$ are dissolved in $5~\mathrm{mL}$ of N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF). The mixture is heated under autogenous pressure for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a Teflonlined autoclave. After the reaction, the autoclave is cooled down to normal temperature, and resulting product is filtered and washed three times with DMF. Thereafter, the white powder is dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and keeps in a dry environment until further use. PVP modified UiO-66s are also prepared using the similar procedure with the feed PVP/Zr4+ molar proportion of 0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1. The final product adopted the soxhlet extraction method with ethanol for 36 hours at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove the remaining redundant PVP in the pores. The final products were denoted as PVP-U-x, where the x represents the PVP/Zr4+ molar ratio.
## 2.2 Characterization
The crystalline structures of samples were characterized using X-ray powder diffraction patterns (XRD, Bruker D8 Advance X-ray diffractometer) Fourier transform infrared spectroscope (FTIR) was carried out by a Nicolet iS10. ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms were analyzed at liquid nitrogen temperature (77 K) with a Quantachrome autosorb-iQ-2MP. Before the measurements, the samples were pretreated under vacuum condition at $105~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 24 h to remove impurities in the channel. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image was collected on a Philips FEIXL-30. Thermal stability of materials was measured by a thermal analyzer (STA8000, PerkinElmer).
## 2.3 Dynamic sorption measurements
The investigations of dynamic adsorption-desorption of toluene vapor were measured in a fixed-bed flow-type apparatus under ordinary pressure. In this work, toluene vapor was produced by bubbling method, it was carried out by pure Argon $(7~\mathrm{mL}~\mathrm{min}^{-1})$ ) in a bubbler. A flow of $30\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ (33mL min-1) and another pure Ar $(10\mathrm{mL}\mathrm{min}^{-1}.\$ ) were employed to dilute and purge toluene vapor (1000 ppm toluene), while flow rate of gas was controlled by mass flow controllers. The adsorption temperature (298K, 323K, 348K, 373K) was controlled by a digital display temperature control furnace. Both inlet and outlet gases were measured by gas chromatograph (GC2060, FID). The relative humidity was provided using a distillated water bubbler, which was placed at the argon stream. The relative humidity ( $30\%$ RH, $50\%$ RH, $70\%$ RH) was controlled by adjusting the flow rate and tested the relative humidity values by a humid meter (HTC-2). The sample powder was carefully pelletized (20 and 40 meshes) before the breakthrough experiments. $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ absorbents were placed in the U-type tubular reactor (6mm outer diameter and $4\mathrm{mm}$ inner diameter). The breakthrough curve of sample was obtained. The adsorption capacity was determined by calculating breakthrough curves areas (Fig. S1) according to the following formula (1):
$$
\mathrm{q}=\frac{\mathrm{t}\times\mathrm{l}-\mathrm{S}}{\mathrm{m}}~\mathrm{C}_{0}\times\mathrm{L}\times10^{-6}
$$
$$
\ln{(\frac{\mathrm{RT_{P}}^{2}}{\upbeta_{\mathrm{H}}})}=\mathrm{\frac{E_{d}}{R}}\mathrm{\frac{1}{T_{P}}}+\ln{(\mathrm{\frac{E_{d}}{K_{0}}})}
$$
2.4 Static adsorption measurements
The static toluene vapor adsorption was performed on a volumetric adsorption apparatus at 298K (Quantachrome Autosorb-IQ-2MP, USA). A vapor generation system was employed to provide toluene vapor at the temperature of $40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The sample cells were putting into water bath in dewar flask to keep constant adsorption temperature. Before the measurement, 60-80 mg samples were pretreated at $105~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for 24h to remove some impurities in the channel. The vapor adsorption isotherms were obtained in $\mathrm{P/P_{0}}$ from 0.005 to 0.8 at 298 K, where $\mathrm{\bfP}$ and $\mathbf{P}_{0}$ were the current and saturated vapor pressures, respectively. Adsorbed amount of toluene was calculated with the ideal gas law by the difference between the initial vapor pressure in sample cell and the pressure at adsorption equilibrium.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Adsorbents Characterization
Fig.1(a) showed the XRD patterns of synthesized UiO-66 and PVP-UiO-66s. The diffraction peaks of all samples fitted well with the crystal patterns reported previously[30], which confirmed that the framework was prepared successfully. The XRD patterns exhibited no significant distinction between the UiO-66 and modified PVP-U- $\mathbf{\sigma}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ samples, indicating that the addition of PVP guest molecules did not obviously affect the crystal structure of UiO-66.
The coordination information of units and zirconium ions could be obtained by FT-IR spectra (Fig. 1(b)). In the case of UiO-66, asymmetric stretch of Zr-(OC) was around $550\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ; 660 and $480~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ were attributed to $\upmu{3}{-}\mathrm{O}$ and $\upmu3$ -OH stretch, respectively[54]. 1800-1300 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to stretched bands of different carboxylate groups vibrations in the benzene rings (organic linkers of MOFs)[54]. The large broad region around $3000{-}3700~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the overlap of the O-H stretching bands of water molecules[55]. With the modification of PVP as structure directing, an obvious change of a peak at $1660^{\circ}\mathrm{{cm}^{\circ}}$ was attributed to the stretching of $\scriptstyle\mathbf{C=O}$ vibrations of carboxylic acid groups and DMF[54]. This peak disappears after using PVP as structure directing, indicating that the $\scriptstyle{C=0}$ ruptured and missing-linker defects might be formed under such circumstances.
The effects of modification on the pore structure of the UiO-66 systems were investigated via ${\bf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms and pore diameter distribution curves (Fig.2). The BET physicochemical parameters were summarized in Table 1. As Fig.2 showed, all the samples gave a similar typical type-I sorption isotherm, the rapid growth at low relative pressure $\mathrm{(P/P_{0}}$ $<0.01)$ was related to the micropore structure. It was found that the addition of PVP lead to the decrease of BET and total pore volume (Table 1), because PVP might affect the self-assembly of organic ligand and metal particles. With the increase of PVP loading, BET and total pore volume both firstly increased and then decreased, which indicated proper PVP loading was conducive to the maintenance of the structure.
The morphology of synthesized samples was further studied via SEM images, respectively. Octahedral microcrystals with particle size over micrometer scales were exhibited for UiO-66 according to Fig.3, which confirmed that solvothermal method provided well crystallized solid state. On the other hand, the particles obtained after PVP modification showed more regular and uniform shapes which were slightly sticky together. It can be clearly identified that the addition of PVP did not affect the morphology, but was beneficial to the formation of regular and uniform morphology.
The thermal gravimetric analysis was performed to observe the thermal stability of all synthesized UiO-66 samples (Fig. S2). UiO-66 samples showed a good thermal stability reported in the literature[56]. The initial weight loss of $4\%$ was observed between $25^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ and $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , which could be accredited to the loss of residual solvent from the pores of the framework. The organic linkers in the frameworks decomposed as the temperature increasing and the structure collapsed at $500~^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ eventually. In relative terms, the thermal stabilities of the newly PVP-U-x were slightly affected by the modification processes. A large weight loss of $10\mathrm{-}15\%$ at $25{\sim}100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was found. Above IR results revealed the benzene ring of organic ligands forms defect sites, which indicated residual solvents were more easily removed from UiO-66 containing defect sites in the process of heating $(25{\sim}100^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . In addition, a further weight loss at ${\sim}500^{\mathrm{{\circ}}}\mathrm{C}$ was observed due to framework decomposition.
## 3.2 Dry adsorption behavior
The dynamic adsorption behavior over UiO-66 and the modified UiO-66s were investigated via breakthrough experiments with an initial toluene concentration of $1000\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . Fig. 4(a) shows the breakthrough curves obtained from toluene adsorption at 298K, for UiO-66 and the modified UiO-66s. The breakthrough time of UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.1/0.3/0.5/0.7/1 represented 133, 175 203, 224 ,189 and $147~\mathrm{min}$ . The adsorption capacities of UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.1/0.3/0.5/0.7/1 were 151, 208, 239, 259, 201 and $173~\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. 4(b)). It was visible that the breakthrough time and adsorption capacity significantly improved with $\mathrm{{PVP/Zr}^{4+}}$ molar ratio increasing up to 0.5 (PVP-U-0.5). However, more PVP dosage resulted in the decrease of adsorption performance. The adsorption capacity PVP-U-0.5 was 1.7 times than that of UiO-66. Compared with previously reported MOFs adsorbents [49, 57], our PVP-U-0.5 displayed higher saturation capacities than those of MOFs adsorbents at relatively low pressure (Table S1).
Fig.S3 presented the static toluene adsorption isotherms of on the UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. It was obviously that the toluene adsorption amount of PVP-U-0.5 was much higher than that of UiO-66. The curves were observed with positive slopes and could be classified as type-I in the IUPAC scheme. This indicated that micropores mainly existed in UiO-66s, which was consistent with the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption results. And micropore filling mechanism occurs mainly in the adsorption process[58]. To better describe the adsorption behavior of toluene vapors onto UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. Freundlich and Langmuir equations were applied to obtain the correlation coefficients and fitting parameters (Table S2). When Langmuir equation was applied, the correlation coefficients $(\mathbf{R}^{2})$ of UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 were 0.927 and 0.901, respectively. When Freundlich equation was applied, the ${\mathrm{\bfR}}^{2}$ of UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 were 0.863 and 0.782, respectively. Thus, the adsorption behaviors of both UiO-66 and PVO-U-0.5 could be better described by the Langmuir equation.
## 3.3. Influence of temperature on adsorption
Different adsorption temperature (298K, 323K, 348K, 373K) were employed to investigated the influence of temperature on adsorption. Fig.5 showed the breakthrough curves of toluene vapor adsorbed over UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. The calculated adsorption amounts were summarized in Table 2. With the increase of temperature, the adsorption of toluene decreased significantly for both UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. In the case of UiO-66, when the temperature rose from 293K to 373K, the adsorption amount of toluene vapor decreased from $151\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ to $68~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . Accordingly, the adsorption of toluene vapor over PVP-U-0.5 decreased from $259\mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}$ to $72~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ . PVP-U-0.5 had greater adsorption advantage at higher adsorption temperature, especially when the temperatures were 323K and 348K, both the adsorption amounts of PVP-U-0.5 were about 2.1 times than that of UiO-66.
In this study, the pressure condition was maintained at a low level $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.0415)$ , Henry’s law constant $(\mathsf{K}_{\mathrm{H}})$ could be chosen to describe the interaction between toluene vapor and the sorbents[49, 59]. The adsorption enthalpy is estimated to provide the information of the bond strengths between the adsorbent surfaces and toluene molecule. Accordingly, an estimation of KH and $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ for single-component could simply be achieved by equation (3) and (4) as follows:
$$
\begin{array}{r l r}&{}&{{\displaystyle{\bf K}_{\mathrm{H}}=\frac{{\bf q}}{{\bf p}\times{\bf M}_{\mathrm{w}}}}~(3)}\\ &{}&{{\displaystyle\mathrm{~\ln~(K_{H})=\frac{\Delta~H_{a d s}}{{\bf R}\mathrm{T}}+\frac{\Delta\mathrm{S}}{{\bf R}^{\prime}}~}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{H}}$ was Henry’s law constant $\mathrm{(mol~kg^{-1}~P a^{-1}}$ ), q was the adsorption capacity (mg $\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ , p was the outlet pressure $\mathrm{(Pa)}$ , and $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{w}}$ was the toluene molecular weight $(\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mol}^{-1};$ ); R was universal gas constant $(8.314,\mathrm{J~mol^{-1}~K^{-1}})$ , T was the adsorption temperature (K), $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ was the adsorption enthalpy $\mathrm{(kJ\mol^{-1}}$ ), and $\Delta S$ was the entropy of adsorption $(\mathrm{J~mol}^{-1}\mathrm{\K}^{-1},$ ).
The corresponding amount of adsorption, ${\sf K}_{\mathrm{H}}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{ads}}$ were calculated in Table 2. Typical physical adsorption behaviors were revealed, while the $\mathrm{\sfK}_{\mathrm{H}}$ values for UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 decreased with the increase of adsorption temperature, ${\sf K}_{\mathrm{H}}$ values of UiO-66 at 298, 323, 348 and 373K were 0.011, 0.006, 0.006 and 0.005, respectively. Accordingly, ${\sf K}_{\mathrm{H}}$ values of PVP-U-0.5 at 298, 323, 348 and 373K were 0.070, 0.050, 0.030 and 0.006, respectively. Higher $\mathrm{K_{H}}$ values of PVP-U-0.5 were obtained, indicating stronger interactions between PVP-U-0.5 and toluene molecules. The enthalpy values of toluene adsorption $\mathrm{\Delta\Delta\Omega_{\mathrm{{Ads}}})}$ computed for UiO-66 and P-U-0.2 were -9.67 and $-28.20\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively. A higher isosteric heat value was observed in PVP-U-0.5, which indicated the stronger interaction between PVP-U-0.5 and toluene molecules. On the other hand, intermolecular $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction formed between toluene molecules when planar aromatic molecules toluene ordered and structural stacked with increasing adsorption capacities[19, 60].
## 3.4. Influence of humidity on adsorption
Water vapor always presents in polluted air and industrial gas emissions, strong competitive adsorption might occur to decrease the VOCs adsorption. Fig.6 showed the working capacities of toluene vapor obtained at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and several relative humidity $(0\%$ , $30\%$ , $50\%$ and $70\%$ RH) for UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. With increasing relative humidity, the adsorption performance of toluene decreased significantly for both UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. In the case of UiO-66, the adsorption amount of toluene vapor at $0\%$ , $30\%$ , $50\%$ and $70\%$ RH were 151, 82, 50 and $27\ \mathrm{\mg\g^{-1}}$ , respectively. Accordingly, the adsorption amount of PVP-U-0.5 at $0\%$ , $30\%$ , $50\%$ and $70\%$ RH were 259, 148, 131 and $84~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}$ , respectively. The decrease of adsorbed toluene amount could be attributed to the adsorption of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecules. The adsorption sites would be occupied by water molecules in the presence of water molecules, it also causes blockages to prevent toluene molecules into the pores to suppress the toluene adsorption. Compared with the pristine UiO-66, it is worth noting that PVP-U-0.5 had higher toluene adsorption capacity. Toluene adsorption capacity of PVP-U-0.5 was three times that of UiO-66 at $70\%$ relative humidity. The lower water uptake of PVP-U-0.5 was ascribed to intensive hydrophobic nature.
3.5 Desorption activation energies (DAE) of toluene and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption
Fig.7(a) and (b) showed the toluene vapor temperature programmed desorption curves over UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 with different heating rates. In each TPD curve, only one peak was observed, which represented toluene desorption peak. The toluene desorption activation energy of UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 were estimated to be 30.23 and $34.09\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ , respectively. A higher toluene DAE was observed on PVP-U-0.5, indicating a higher interaction between PVP-U-0.5 and toluene molecules. Fig.7 (c) and (d) showed water vapor TPD curves on UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 with different heating rates. Only one single peak occurred for each TPD curves, which revealed relative homogenous surface properties towards $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules at moderate coverage. The water desorption temperatures of PVP-U-0.5 were lower than that of UiO-66, indicating that lower energy was required to release $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule from the
PVP-U-0.5. The DAE of water molecules on UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 were further calculated, 19.92 and $11.51\mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}$ were observed, respectively. This revealed that PVP-U-0.5 had more hydrophobic property after modifying with PVP.
Fig.S4 gave adsorption capacity values and regeneration cycles over regenerated UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 after the TPD research and for three cycles. The adsorption capacity values of UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5 were 151, 149, 153, and 259, 254, 251 mg g-1, respectively. It was calculated that the adsorption processes and TPD experiment had no significant effect on the adsorption performance of both UiO-66 and PVP-U-0.5. Further confirmed that the adsorption mechanism was physical adsorption and toluene can be desorbed by heating or pressurizing to regenerate the materials.
## 4. Conclusion
On the basis of the previously studies on the adsorption of toluene over UiO-66 [20, 22, 37, 39, 40, 49, 61], this work had been performed aiming at developing more effective and selective adsorbent materials. The application of PVP in the preparation process could be used as a surfactant to regulate the morphology, shape, size of materials. Therefore, this paper functionalized new UiO- structures with PVP as structure-directing agent based on UiO-66. The modified defective UiO-66 shows similar morphology and crystal structure in comparison to the UiO-66. The breakthrough experiments, toluene vapor adsorption isotherm and toluene and TPD measurements were preformed to investigate the adsorption capacity and behavior of toluene vapor over UiO-66 and modified samples. It was found that the modified material has the highest adsorption capacity of $259\ \mathrm{mg\g^{-1}}$ for toluene while PVP/ ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ was 0.5, which demonstrated 1.7 times of UiO-66. Moreover, a higher isosteric heat value was observed in PVP-U-0.5, indicating the stronger interaction between PVP-U-0.5 and toluene molecules. Compared with previous reported adsorption toluene results of MOFs [20, 22, 37, 39, 40, 49, 61], PVP-U-0.5 has good adsorption capacity at high humidity. Water-TPD experiments revealed that PVP-U-0.5 had more hydrophobic property after using PVP as structure directing agent, which provided a further possibility for practical application. Further work will be study the changes of MOFs structure through theoretical calculation and the mechanism of toluene adsorption in the presence of water in detail.
